WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.873
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.173 --> 00:00:09.594
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.894 --> 00:00:16.238
Unit twelve: Regional Trade Blocs. Today you will use fixed prepositions after verbs, nouns and adjectives.

4
00:00:16.538 --> 00:00:19.121
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.903
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:25.856
Free trade zone.

7
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:31.334
Free trade zone means an area where goods move between members without tariffs.

8
00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.484
The free trade zone boosted border towns.

9
00:00:35.083 --> 00:00:37.736
Your turn. Say: Free trade zone.

10
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:41.973
Free trade zone.

11
00:00:42.717 --> 00:00:43.857
Common market.

12
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:48.986
Common market means a bloc in which goods, workers and money move freely.

13
00:00:49.583 --> 00:00:53.046
A common market lets citizens work in any member country.

14
00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:56.337
Your turn. Say: Common market.

15
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.294
Common market.

16
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.469
Regional integration.

17
00:01:03.483 --> 00:01:08.351
Regional integration means the process of neighbouring countries becoming economically closer.

18
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.530
Regional integration took thirty years.

19
00:01:12.117 --> 00:01:15.233
Your turn. Say: Regional integration.

20
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.716
Regional integration.

21
00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:21.625
Customs union.

22
00:01:22.617 --> 00:01:27.507
Customs union means a bloc whose members share the same tariffs on outside goods.

23
00:01:28.117 --> 00:01:31.115
The customs union set one tariff for all imports.

24
00:01:31.717 --> 00:01:34.752
Your turn. Say: Customs union.

25
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:38.787
Customs union.

26
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:41.074
Trade bloc membership.

27
00:01:42.083 --> 00:01:46.753
Trade bloc membership means the status of belonging to a group of trading partners.

28
00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:50.627
Trade bloc membership requires accepting common rules.

29
00:01:51.217 --> 00:01:54.751
Your turn. Say: Trade bloc membership.

30
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.205
Trade bloc membership.

31
00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.392
Economic community.

32
00:02:02.383 --> 00:02:07.518
Economic community means a group of countries cooperating closely on economic policy.

33
00:02:08.117 --> 00:02:11.417
The economic community agreed on a joint budget.

34
00:02:12.017 --> 00:02:14.866
Your turn. Say: Economic community.

35
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.340
Economic community.

36
00:02:20.083 --> 00:02:21.526
Cross-border trade.

37
00:02:22.517 --> 00:02:27.094
Cross-border trade means buying and selling goods across national frontiers.

38
00:02:27.683 --> 00:02:30.391
Cross-border trade doubled in five years.

39
00:02:30.983 --> 00:02:34.483
Your turn. Say: Cross-border trade.

40
00:02:37.333 --> 00:02:39.147
Cross-border trade.

41
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:41.546
Regional cooperation.

42
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:46.918
Regional cooperation means neighbouring countries working together on shared goals.

43
00:02:47.517 --> 00:02:50.573
Regional cooperation improved port security.

44
00:02:51.183 --> 00:02:54.509
Your turn. Say: Regional cooperation.

45
00:02:57.367 --> 00:02:59.157
Regional cooperation.

46
00:02:59.967 --> 00:03:04.105
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:11.886
Visal, a report says 'Smaller economies often depend on access to larger markets.' Why 'depend on' and 'access to'?

48
00:03:12.983 --> 00:03:25.133
Many verbs, nouns and adjectives need a fixed preposition: depend on, access to, benefit from, dependence on, member of, committed to. Learn these collocations together.

49
00:03:26.217 --> 00:03:27.496
Is there a pattern?

50
00:03:28.583 --> 00:03:43.394
Partly. Nouns often keep the preposition of their verb: depend on → dependence on, contribute to → contribution to. But 'access to' has no verb, so you must memorise it.

51
00:03:44.483 --> 00:03:46.727
What if a verb follows the preposition?

52
00:03:47.817 --> 00:03:59.165
Then use -ing: 'committed to reducing tariffs', 'benefit from trading freely', 'interested in joining the bloc'. Never use an infinitive after a preposition.

53
00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:01.893
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:06.096
Grammar focus: Dependent prepositions plus noun and -ing.

55
00:04:06.396 --> 00:04:07.595
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:08.283 --> 00:04:11.630
Small economies depend on access to larger markets.

57
00:04:12.517 --> 00:04:15.352
Members are committed to reducing internal tariffs.

58
00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:19.271
Farmers benefit from trading freely across borders.

59
00:04:20.233 --> 00:04:28.493
Learn fixed prepositions: depend on, access to, benefit from, committed to. After a preposition use a noun or -ing, never to + verb.

60
00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.377
Be careful! Don't say: She is interested to join the bloc.

61
00:04:35.333 --> 00:04:38.192
Say: She is interested in joining the bloc.

62
00:04:38.492 --> 00:04:40.782
Use in + -ing after interested.

63
00:04:41.567 --> 00:04:44.056
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:44.867 --> 00:04:46.228
Stronger Together.

65
00:04:46.528 --> 00:04:50.594
A regional trade bloc connects neighbouring countries through shared rules.

66
00:04:51.600 --> 00:05:00.148
A free trade zone removes tariffs on cross-border trade, while a customs union also adopts one set of tariffs against outsiders.

67
00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:08.601
A common market goes further by allowing workers and capital to move freely, and an economic community may share even more policies.

68
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:13.573
Members are often committed to deepening regional integration step by step.

69
00:05:14.567 --> 00:05:21.082
Smaller countries benefit from joining because they gain access to larger markets and a stronger voice in regional cooperation.

70
00:05:22.167 --> 00:05:26.145
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:26.983 --> 00:05:30.225
Smaller states benefit blank regional cooperation.

72
00:05:30.525 --> 00:05:33.389
A, of. B, from. C, to.

73
00:05:36.933 --> 00:05:38.631
The answer is B: from.

74
00:05:39.783 --> 00:05:44.929
A group that removes tariffs among members and sets common outside tariffs is a customs blank.

75
00:05:45.229 --> 00:05:48.558
A, union. B, dispute. C, embargo.

76
00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.844
The answer is A: union.

77
00:05:54.983 --> 00:05:56.704
Choose the correct sentence.

78
00:05:57.004 --> 00:06:04.721
A, They are committed to reduce tariffs.. B, They are committed to reducing tariffs.. C, They are committed for reducing tariffs..

79
00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:11.810
The answer is B: They are committed to reducing tariffs..

80
00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:16.203
Trade across a border between two countries is blank trade.

81
00:06:16.503 --> 00:06:20.006
A, cross-border. B, domestic. C, local.

82
00:06:23.567 --> 00:06:25.845
The answer is A: cross-border.

83
00:06:26.983 --> 00:06:30.492
According to the reading, what do smaller countries gain access to?

84
00:06:30.792 --> 00:06:35.398
A, larger markets. B, new borders. C, fewer rules.

85
00:06:38.933 --> 00:06:41.792
The answer is A: larger markets.

86
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:46.163
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:47.017 --> 00:06:51.257
What are the benefits of joining a regional trade bloc for a smaller country?

88
00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:01.515
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:01.815 --> 00:07:24.300
A smaller country gains access to larger markets without paying high tariffs, which helps its exporters grow. It can also attract investment, share costs with neighbours and speak with a stronger voice in negotiations. However, it must accept common rules, so membership works best when the bloc treats all members fairly.

90
00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.484
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:27.383 --> 00:07:32.590
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and dependent prepositions plus noun and -ing.

92
00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:38.596
Today's value is collaboration. Work together, and share both the effort and the success.

93
00:07:38.896 --> 00:07:41.954
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:42.254 --> 00:07:44.825
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

